{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST parse Rust, Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, and YAML\nsources with pinned tree-sitter grammars, selected by file extension. Every\nother text file MUST use a plain-text profile in which each line that is not\nblank is an anonymous outline node of kind `line`. Each supported language MUST\nhave an outline profile that maps grammar node kinds to short outline kind names\nand, for each mapped kind, states whether a node is named, and by which fields\nor children, or anonymous. Only mapped kinds MUST appear in the outline; other\nnodes MUST be transparent, so their outline descendants appear under the\nnearest outline ancestor. A profile MAY attach grammar siblings to an outline\nnode, such as Rust attributes and doc comments preceding an item, so the outline\ntree need not match the grammar tree exactly; attached siblings are part of that\nnode for its line range and hash. Every file's outline MUST have a root node\nfor the whole file. Comments MUST NOT be outline nodes. Profiles MUST be\ndocumented. Upgrading a grammar or profile MAY change outlines and MUST be\ntreated as a deliberate change.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: false.** Most of the requirement is implemented, documented and tested. One implemented behavior contradicts its text, and no other definition specifies that behavior.\n\n**What is satisfied**\n- **Grammars and profiles.** The seven languages are parsed with exactly pinned grammars (`Cargo.toml` lines 62–69, e.g. `tree-sitter-rust = \"=0.24.2\"`). Profile selection is by extension in `profile_for_path`. Each language has a `Profile` and `KINDS` table with `NameRule` variants (`Field`, `FirstChild`, `Custom`, `Anonymous`) in `src/outline/{rust,python,typescript,css,markdown,toml,yaml}.rs`.\n- **Plain text.** `plain_text` in `src/outline/mod.rs` makes each non-blank line an anonymous `line` node, and `plain.rs` supplies the profile.\n- **Root node.** Every outline gets a root node (`root_node`).\n- **Comments.** Comments are excluded from mapping (`Builder::maps` checks `!is_comment`) and are recorded separately.\n- **Attachment.** Attached siblings (Rust attributes and outer doc comments, Python decorators) are handled in `Builder::walk`, and the range and hash include them.\n- **Documentation.** `md/reference.md` \"Outlines\" and \"Outline profiles\" give a per-language table. It also states that grammar versions are pinned and that upgrades are deliberate.\n- **Tests.** `src/outline/tests.rs` has a test per profile (Python, TypeScript/TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML/YAML), a plain-text test, a parse-failure test, and line-query tests. `src/authoring/tools.rs` has a test of the `outline` tool. Rust-profile behavior is covered by the selector requirements.\n\n**Gap**\n- **Fallback nodes.** `Builder::walk` in `src/outline/mod.rs` has a branch (`parent == 0 && child.is_named() && !self.contains_mapped(child)`). It creates an anonymous node of `profile.fallback` kind (`stmt`, `item`, `block`, `node`) for any top-level grammar node that contains no mapped node.\n- **Why it matters.** The tests assert these nodes: `stmt@ 1-1` for Python, `block@ 1-3` for Markdown front matter, `stmt@ 6-6` for CSS `@import`.\n- **Conflict with the requirement.** It says \"Only mapped kinds MUST appear in the outline; other nodes MUST be transparent\". The fallback nodes are unmapped grammar nodes that appear in the outline. The requirement does not mention a fallback kind.\n- **No other definition covers it.** A search of `md/requirements` finds no requirement describing fallback or top-level behavior. The behavior appears only as prose in `md/reference.md`, which is not a requirement. The `fallback` field on `Profile` and the walk branch are marked only with `requirements.references.outlines`, so the code carries behavior that no requirement specifies.\n\nEither the requirement should be amended to define the fallback kind, or the code should drop it. Until then the implementation does not match the requirement exactly. No FIXME acknowledges this gap, so the answer is false rather than FIXME.\n\n**Minor note.** The Rust `KINDS` table is marked `{impl}` only for `rust_item_selectors`, not `outlines`. `PROFILE` carries the `outlines` mark, so I do not count this as a gap.",
  "inputs": [
